1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:11,250
GenSPARQL extends SPARQL with GENOP, an operator that binds values generated by a large language model to query variables during query evaluation.

2
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:16,950
A similarity join maps the generated names to the entities of the graph.

3
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:24,375
The query planner invokes the LLM only for the bindings that the other graph patterns and filters select.

4
00:00:25,867 --> 00:00:33,492
The workbench shows a graph of the 2026 World Cup and a graph of Nobel laureates, both built from Wikidata.

5
00:00:33,942 --> 00:00:40,167
On the project page, its recorded mode replays the answers of three open-weight LLMs.

6
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:44,242
The graph stores no positions of the players.

7
00:00:44,692 --> 00:00:51,492
A context-mode GENOP sends one prompt for each Argentine player and binds the generated position.

8
00:00:51,942 --> 00:01:01,267
The workbench compares the positions that Qwen3-30B, Qwen3-8B and Mistral-7B generated with the positions in Wikidata.

9
00:01:03,967 --> 00:01:09,592
A base-mode GENOP sends a single prompt that lists the national teams of the World Cup.

10
00:01:10,042 --> 00:01:17,267
The similarity join maps each generated name to every team with a label of similarity at least θ.

11
00:01:17,717 --> 00:01:24,967
At θ = 0.85, United States stays unmapped, because the graph labels the team USA.

12
00:01:25,417 --> 00:01:35,692
With the embedding similarity at θ = 0.8, the mapping view adds only incorrect matches, such as Australia to USA and Sweden to Norway.

13
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:44,050
This query asks for the players of Group A whose generated position is defender.

14
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:51,175
With GENOP first, the LLM is called once for each of the 1,248 players.

15
00:01:51,625 --> 00:02:01,625
The planner first evaluates the patterns that select Group A, so the LLM is called only for its 104 players, twelve times fewer calls.

16
00:02:03,333 --> 00:02:08,433
In the live mode, the service evaluates each query with the chosen LLM.

17
00:02:08,883 --> 00:02:16,833
Here, the service and Ollama with Mistral-7B and bge-large-en-v1.5 run on this laptop, without a provider account.

18
00:02:17,283 --> 00:02:23,783
The query asks for the position of each Argentine player, so the service sends one prompt per player.

19
00:02:27,271 --> 00:02:34,496
The answers appear in the result table, and the workbench reports 26 LLM calls, one per player.

20
00:02:34,946 --> 00:02:39,146
Attendees can also edit the sample queries or write their own.

21
00:02:39,596 --> 00:02:45,496
This edit asks for the players of Japan, and the service again sends one prompt per player.

22
00:02:48,718 --> 00:02:52,468
The positions of the Japanese players appear in the table.

23
00:02:54,367 --> 00:03:02,067
The project page replays the recorded runs, and the repository contains the code, the queries and the evaluation.

